From af6b987ca61c4c8621909f41243de7062610a468 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bram Cohen <bram@chia.net>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 16:47:47 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Decide ties in Boolean3 by one finite move of the vertices

Two values within TieTol are a tie, and Shadows broke each by a direction
of its own: vertex normals for x, an edge's face normals for y, face
normals for z. Those don't describe one configuration, and where coplanar
faces of P and Q share their normals, the direction is exactly 0 or a
rounding error's worth either side of it, so the decision follows how the
normals happen to round. Batch unions of convex pieces with coplanar faces
(the faces of a voxel shape swept by a cube) are left with zero-volume
flaps of overlapping triangles.

Now each tie is decided by one move of all the vertices: by a quarter of
the tolerance along a displacement that keeps flat faces flat, the verts of
Q outward, those of P outward when expanding and inward otherwise and an
eighth further (so that where faces of P and Q coincide, P's is the outer
one), and P shifted a little more for what is left. At a tie the two values
are taken as equal, and the comparison is decided by how much the move
changes each. The move is one real configuration, so the ties are all
decided alike; it is far larger than the rounding that turns values which
should be equal into ties, and too small to change a comparison that isn't
a tie. Shadows is no longer used and is removed.

Tests: CoplanarBatchUnion and CollinearEdgesBatchUnion compare a batch union
of swept face pieces with the same union taken one piece at a time; the
first fails on master. CubeUnionProp and MeshGLRoundTrip get 18 verts / 32
tris (the corner of the upper of two coplanar faces from different meshes
stays a vertex of the seam), Simplify's RemoveDegenerates 34.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
